course: dictionary
lesson: 10439
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word troy is a good example. «Troy was a real city in what is now Turkey.» Here Troy is a place name, so it starts with a capital letter.
explain: In «the wooden horse of Troy», the word belongs to an old story from Greek poems. A good historian asks: is this a true record, or a legend? Troy was real, but the wooden horse comes from legend.
explain: In «troy weight» and «troy ounce», troy is a small letter word about weighing gold, silver and gems. The words after it, weight and ounce, are your clues. Always check the words around an unknown word.
fact: Troy is a proper noun: it is the name of a place or a person, so it starts with a capital letter.
fact: Troy was an ancient city in what is now Turkey, famous from Greek stories of the Trojan War.
fact: In the Greek stories, the Greeks got inside the city by hiding in a giant wooden horse.
fact: A «Trojan horse» can also mean a trick to get inside, or a computer virus hidden in a harmless-looking file.
fact: Troy is also a boy's name, and a troy ounce is an old unit for weighing gold, silver and gems, a little heavier than an ordinary ounce.
exercise: «Troy was an ancient city in what is now Turkey.» What kind of word is Troy here? | A weight for gold | *A place name | A boy's name | A type of horse
exercise: In «troy weight», what is the system used for? | Weighing people at the market | Measuring rain | *Weighing gold, silver and gems | Naming a horse
exercise: In the Greek stories, how did the Greeks get inside Troy? | Through a dark cave | A tall stone wall | By sailing over the sea in gold ships | *By hiding in a giant wooden horse
test: «Troy was a real city, but the wooden horse is a legend.» Is the wooden horse true history or a legend? | A recent news report | Proven history from a museum | *A legend from old poems | A real horse from Turkey
test: A computer virus hidden in a harmless-looking file is sometimes called what? | A minotaur | A troy ounce | A Greek wall | *A Trojan horse
exam: A jeweller says, «Gold is sold by the troy ounce.» What does troy tell you? | *An old weighing system, a little heavier than an ordinary ounce | A wooden horse from an old story | The name of a Greek city in a poem | A boy's name
